          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Where does guidance differ on non-antibiotic management of uncomplicated UTI?",
          "answer": "The retrieved passages do not answer this question. None of them describe non-antibiotic management of uncomplicated UTI or where guidance disagrees about it.\n\nThe only relevant source was the *Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence* guidance from an infectious diseases and urology society (educational summary). The passage returned from it covers who the guidance applies to and when testing is needed. It says that in a non-pregnant adult with typical symptoms and no vaginal discharge or irritation, treatment can start without a urine culture. It also says a urine dipstick adds little in that group. That guidance has a section on where bodies disagree, but the passage from it was not returned, so I cannot tell you what that section says.\n\nThe other results were about osteoarthritis, upper respiratory infections, diabetes and hypertension. They do not apply to this question.\n\nA narrower search, such as \"self-care or symptom relief for uncomplicated cystitis\" or \"the disagreement section of the uncomplicated UTI guidance\", might find the relevant passages. For decisions about your own care, a clinician or pharmacist can explain which options suit your situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
